/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776906","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Monitoring forest carbon (C) stocks is essential to better assess their role in the global carbon balance, and to better model and predict long-term trends and inter-annual variability in atmospheric CO2 concentrations. On a national scale, national forest inventories (NFIs) can provide estimates of forest carbon stocks, but these estimates are only available in certain countries, are limited by time lags due to periodic revisits, and cannot provide spatially continuous mapping of forests. In this context, remote sensing offers many advantages for monitoring above-ground biomass (AGB) on a global scale with good spatial (50–100 m) and temporal (annual) resolutions. Remote sensing has been used for several decades to monitor vegetation. However, traditional methods of monitoring AGB using optical or microwave sensors are affected by saturation effects for moderately or densely vegetated canopies, limiting their performance. Low-frequency passive microwave remote sensing is less affected by these saturation effects: saturation only occurs at AGB levels of around 400 t/ha at L-band (frequency of around 1.4 GHz). Despite its coarse spatial resolution of the order of 25 km × 25 km, this method based on the L-VOD (vegetation optical depth at L-band) index has recently established itself as an essential approach for monitoring annual variations in forest AGB on a continental scale. Thus, L-VOD has been applied to forest monitoring in many continents and biomes: in the tropics (especially in the Amazon and Congo basins), in boreal regions (Siberia, Canada), in Europe, China, Australia, etc. However, no reference study has yet been published to analyze L-VOD in detail in terms of capabilities, validation and results. This paper fills this gap by presenting the physical principles of L-VOD calculation, analyzing the performance of L-VOD for monitoring AGB and reviewing the main applications of L-VOD for tracking the carbon balance of global vegetation over the last decade (2010–2019).","author_count":22,"contributors":["Wigneron, Jean-Pierre","Ciais, Philippe","Li, Xiaojun","Brandt, Martin","Canadell, Josep G.","Tian, Feng","Wang, Huan","Bastos, Ana","Fan, Lei","Gatica, Gabriel","Kashyap, Rahul","Liu, Xiangzhuo","Sitch, Stephen","Tao, Shengli","Xiao, Xiangming","Yang, Hui","Villar, Jhan Carlo Espinoza","Frappart, Frederic","Li, Wei","Qin, Yuanwei","De Truchis, Aurélien","Fensholt, Rasmus"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-777713"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","America, South","Asia, East","Asia, South","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Argentina","Australia","China","France","Germany","India","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CSIRO","INRAE","Indian Institute of Technology Kharagpur","Institute of Environmental Geosciences","Kayrros SAS (Biz)","Ludwig Maximilian University of Munich","Max Planck Institute for Biogeochemistry","Paris-Saclay University","Peking University","Southwest University","Tsinghua University","Universidad Nacional de San Juan","University of Exeter","University of Oklahoma","Wuhan University"],"keyword":["Biomass","Forest","Global carbon cycle","Passive microwave"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"global carbon balance of the forest satellite based l vod results over the last decade","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frsen.2024.1338618","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-777713","identifier":[{"id":"85193942083","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-533abd0f-2c4f-4f8a-b050-77a71c8f3944"],"keyword":["L-VOD","biomass","forest","global carbon cycle","passive microwave"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B7973629544325EFF0B52E2621F70D32"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/frsen.2024.1338618","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/413232101/frsen_05_1338618.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"fr","key":"inrae","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"INRAE","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fr","key":"paris saclay university","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Paris-Saclay University","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Geosciences and Natural Resource Management, Geography, Land, Environment and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"au","key":"csiro","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"CSIRO","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"cn","key":"wuhan university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Wuhan University","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"cn","key":"peking university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Peking University","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"de","key":"ludwig maximilian university of munich","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Ludwig Maximilian University of Munich","used":1},{"affno":"8","country":"cn","key":"southwest university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Southwest University","used":1},{"affno":"9","country":"ar","key":"universidad nacional de san juan","loc":["Argentina","America, South"],"name":"Universidad Nacional de San Juan","used":1},{"affno":"10","country":"in","key":"indian institute of technology kharagpu