, Jiefang","rank":21,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"8","first":"Karsten","key":"kristiansenkarstenpau","last":"Kristiansen","locid":"941041b1-1b8f-4be5-9a65-5e98f662c07e","name":"Kristiansen, Karsten","orcid":"0000-0002-6024-0917","rank":22,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Xin","key":"jinxinpau","last":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xin","rank":23,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Xun","key":"xuxunpau","last":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xun","rank":24,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Minfeng","key":"xiaominfengpau","last":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Minfeng","rank":25,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7","first":"Bernard","key":"agwandabernardpau","last":"Agwanda","name":"Agwanda, Bernard","rank":26,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"9","first":"Sheila","key":"ommehsheilapau","last":"Ommeh","name":"Ommeh, Sheila","rank":27,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Junhua","key":"lijunhuapau","last":"Li","name":"Li, Junhua","rank":28,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 10","first":"Zheng Li","key":"shizhenglipau","last":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zheng Li","rank":29,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38600530"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s40168-024-01782-4","issn":["20492618"],"issue":"1","journal":"Microbiome","paperid":"72","status":"p","title":"Microbiome","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85189888408"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-23e461ae-9374-4394-8a1b-a5839f5033e5"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Substantial viral diversity in bats and rodents from East Africa : insights into evolution, recombination, and cocirculation","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-730634","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The barley powdery mildew fungus, Blumeria hordei (Bh), secretes hundreds of candidate secreted effector proteins (CSEPs) to facilitate pathogen infection and colonization. One of these, CSEP0008, is directly recognized by the barley nucleotide-binding leucine-rich-repeat (NLR) receptor MLA1 and therefore is designated AVRA1. Here, we show that AVRA1 and the sequence-unrelated Bh effector BEC1016 (CSEP0491) suppress immunity in barley. We used yeast two-hybrid next-generation interaction screens (Y2H-NGIS), followed by binary Y2H and in planta protein–protein interactions studies, and identified a common barley target of AVRA1 and BEC1016, the endoplasmic reticulum (ER)-localized J-domain protein HvERdj3B. Silencing of this ER quality control (ERQC) protein increased Bh penetration. HvERdj3B is ER luminal, and we showed using split GFP that AVRA1 and BEC1016 translocate into the ER signal peptide-independently. Overexpression of the two effectors impeded trafficking of a vacuolar marker through the ER; silencing of HvERdj3B also exhibited this same cellular phenotype, coinciding with the effectors targeting this ERQC component. Together, these results suggest that the barley innate immunity, preventing Bh entry into epidermal cells, requires ERQC. Here, the J-domain protein HvERdj3B appears to be essential and can be regulated by AVRA1 and BEC1016. Plant disease resistance often occurs upon direct or indirect recognition of pathogen effectors by host NLR receptors. Previous work has shown that AVRA1 is directly recognized in the cytosol by the immune receptor MLA1. We speculate that the AVRA1 J-domain target being inside the ER, where it is inapproachable by NLRs, has forced the plant to evolve this challenging direct recognition.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Li, Zizhang","Velásquez-Zapata, Valeria","Elmore, J. Mitch","Li, Xuan","Xie, Wenjun","Deb, Sohini","Tian, Xiao","Banerjee, Sagnik","Jørgensen, Hans Jørgen Lyngs","Pedersen, Carsten","Wise, Roger P.","Thordal-Christensen, Hans"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-740057"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Corn Insects and Crop Genetics Research Unit","Iowa State University"],"keyword":["Barley","ER quality control","ERdj3b","Immunity","Pathogen effectors","Powdery mildew","signal peptide independent ER uptake","yeast two hybrid next generation interaction screening"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"powdery mildew effectors avr sub a000000000001 sub and bec000000001016 target the er j domain protein i hv i erdj000000000003b required for immunity in barley","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/mpp.13463","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-740057","identifier":[{"id":"38695677","type":"pmid"},{"id":"001215977500001","type":"wos"},{"id":"PMC11064805","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85192035338","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-f0a949b6-aee9-44b2-9a0f-b92d966f8f3a"],"keyword":["ER quality control","ERdj3b","barley","immunity","pathogen effectors","powdery mildew","signal peptide-independent ER-uptake","yeast two-hybrid next-generation interaction screening"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1276F9DF28BF37BEFA2B2ABB27AE5D8D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/mpp.13463","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/391744157/Molecular_Plant_Pathology_2024_Li_Powdery_mildew_effectors_AVRA1_and_BEC1016_target_the_ER_J_domain_protein_HvERdj3B.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, Section for Plant and Soil Sciences"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"iowa state university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Iowa State University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"corn insects and crop genetics research unit","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Corn Insects and Crop Genetics Research Unit","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Zizhang","key":"lizizhangpau","last":"Li","locid":"a470deab-a3da-4c0f-a882-83026248cbad","name":"Li, Zizhang","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Valeria","key":"velásquezzapatavaleriapau","last":"Velásquez-Zapata","name":"Velásquez-Zapata, Valeria","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"J. Mitch","key":"elmorejmitchpau","last":"Elmore","name":"Elmore, J. Mitch","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Xuan","key":"lixuanpau","last":"Li","locid":"2b8054a3-f72e-4c23-9da5-c547c75ebb07","name":"Li, Xuan","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Wenjun","key":"xiewenjunpau","last":"Xie","locid":"2575d7cd-b9f4-4383-a34f-d1d98c7284a8","name":"Xie, Wenjun","orcid":"0000-0001-8101-5519","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Sohini","key":"debsohinipau","last":"Deb","locid":"c57688f1-092a-44ad-b4d9-7d0965c8e0e8","name":"Deb, Sohini","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Xiao","key":"tianxiaopau","last":"Tian","locid":"6c7da93d-bf14-404b-8b6f-a78f913c7e22","name":"Tian, Xiao","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Sagnik","key":"banerjeesagnikpau","last":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Sagnik","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hans J.L.","key":"jørgensenhansjørgenlyngspau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"1b3f39b0-a166-4a0c-96ea-e6c3dd0de338","name":"Jørgensen, Hans Jørgen Lyngs","name_org":"Jørgensen, Hans J.L.","orcid":"0000-0002-5902-6550","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Carsten","key":"pedersencarstenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"7750ab11-4cc4-4421-8073-4a339254a400","name":"Pedersen, Carsten","orcid":"0000-0002-9520-2232","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Roger P.","key":"wiserogerppau","last":"Wise","name":"Wise, Roger P.","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hans","key":"thordalchristensenhanspau","last":"Thordal-Christensen","locid":"3e904207-07a2-412e-969f-3272f1fe12c5","name":"Thordal-Christensen, Hans","orcid":"0000-0001-8813-0157","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38695677"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/mpp.13463","issn":["13643703","14646722"],"issn_org":["14646722"],"issue":"5","journal":"Molecular Plant Pathology","paperid":"e13463","status":"p","title":"Molecular Plant Pathology","volume":"25"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85192035338"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-f0a949b6-aee9-44b2-9a0f-b92d966f8f3a"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Powdery mildew effectors AVR<sub>A1</sub> and BEC1016 target the ER J-domain protein <i>Hv</i>ERdj3B required for immunity in barley","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-740057","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The development of biomaterials capable of regulating cellular processes and guiding cell fate decisions has broad implications in tissue engineering, regenerative medicine, and cell-based assays for drug development and disease modeling. Recent studies have shown that three-dimensional (3D) nanoscale physical cues such as nanotopography can modulate various cellular processes like adhesion and endocytosis by inducing nanoscale curvature on the plasma and nuclear membranes. Two-dimensional (2D) biochemical cues such as protein micropatterns can also regulate cell function and fate by controlling cellular geometries. Development of biomaterials with precise control over nanoscale physical and biochemical cues can significantly influence programming cell function and fate. In this study, we utilized a laser-assisted micropatterning technique to manipulate the 2D architectures of cells on 3D nanopillar platforms. We performed a comprehensive analysis of cellular and nuclear morphology and deformation on both nanopillar and flat substrates. Our findings demonstrate the precise engineering of single cell architectures through 2D micropatterning on nanopillar platforms. We show that the coupling between the nuclear and cell shape is disrupted on nanopillar surfaces compared to flat surfaces. Furthermore, our results suggest that cell elongation on nanopillars enhances nanopillar-induced endocytosis. We believe our platform serves as a versatile tool for further explorations into programming cell function and fate through combined physical cues that create nanoscale curvature on cell membranes and biochemical cues that control the geometry of the cell.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Sarikhani, Einollah","Meganathan, Dhivya Pushpa","Larsen, Anne Kathrine Kure","Rahmani, Keivan","Tsai, Ching Ting","Lu, Chih Hao","Marquez-Serrano, Abel","Sadr, Leah","Li, Xiao","Dong, Mingdong","Santoro, Francesca","Cui, Bianxiao","Klausen, Lasse Hyldgaard","Jahed, Zeinab"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-752601"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Jahed, Zeinab","Klausen, Lasse Hyldgaard"],"country":["Germany","Italy","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Italian Institute of Technology","Jülich Research Centre ( FZJ )","RWTH Aachen University","Stanford University","University of California at San Diego"],"keyword":["Mechanobiology","Micropatterning","Nanopillar","Nanostructures"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"engineering the cellular microenvironment integrating three dimensional nontopographical and two dimensional biochemical cues for precise control of cellular behavior","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acsnano.4c03743","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-752601","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11271182","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"38978500","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85198076629","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-0e24d348-fc41-435f-a787-b61dfdd69e00"],"keyword":["cell and nuclear deformation","cell and nuclear mechanics","cell and nuclear morphology","mechanobiology","micropatterning","nanopillar","nanostructures"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DFE70B22438EED1D62934560556A5BF7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acsnano.4c03743","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of california at san diego","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California at San Diego","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"a