{"abstract":"Introduction: Mosaic chromosomal alterations (mCAs) detected in white blood cells represent a type of clonal hematopoiesis (CH) that is understudied compared with CH-related somatic mutations. A few recent studies indicated their potential link with nonhematological cancers, especially lung cancer. Methods: In this study, we investigated the association between mCAs and lung cancer using the high-density genotyping data from the OncoArray study of INTEGRAL-ILCCO, the largest single genetic study of lung cancer with 18,221 lung cancer cases and 14,825 cancer-free controls. Results: We identified a comprehensive list of autosomal mCAs, ChrX mCAs, and mosaic ChrY (mChrY) losses from these samples. Autosomal mCAs were detected in 4.3% of subjects, in addition to ChrX mCAs in 3.6% of females and mChrY losses in 9.6% of males. Multivariable logistic regression analysis indicated that the presence of autosomal mCAs in white blood cells was associated with an increased lung cancer risk after adjusting for key confounding factors, including age, sex, smoking status, and race. This association was mainly driven by a specific type of mCAs: copy-neutral loss of heterozygosity on autosomal chromosomes. The association between autosome copy-neutral loss of heterozygosity and increased risk of lung cancer was further confirmed in two major histologic subtypes, lung adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma. In addition, we observed a significant increase of ChrX mCAs and mChrY losses in smokers compared with nonsmokers and racial differences in certain types of mCA events. Conclusions: Our study established a link between mCAs in white blood cells and increased risk of lung cancer.","author_count":46,"contributors":["Cheng, Chao","Hong, Wei","Li, Yafang","Xiao, Xiangjun","McKay, James","Han, Younghun","Byun, Jinyoung","Peng, Bo","Albanes, Demetrios","Lam, Stephen","Tardon, Adonina","Chen, Chu","Bojesen, Stig E","Landi, Maria T.","Johansson, Mattias","Risch, Angela","Bickeböller, Heike","Wichmann, H.-Erich","Christiani, David C.","Rennert, Gad","Arnold, Susanne","Goodman, Gary","Field, John K.","Davies, Michael P. A.","Shete, Sanjay S.","Le Marchand, Loic","Liu, Geoffrey","Hung, Rayjean J.","Andrew, Angeline S.","Kiemeney, Lambertus A.","Zhu, Meng","Shen, Hongbing","Zienolddiny, Shan","Grankvist, Kjell","Johansson, Mikael","Cox, Angela","Hong, Yun-Chul","Yuan, Jian-Min","Lazarus, Philip","Schabath, Matthew B.","Aldrich, Melinda C.","Brennan, Paul","Li, Yong","Gorlova, Olga","Gorlov, Ivan","Amos, Christopher I."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-690226"],"filters":{"added":"2023-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Asia, Middle East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corpau":["International Lung Cancer Consortium (INTEGRAL-ILCCO)"],"country":["Austria","Canada","China","France","Germany","Israel","Netherlands","Norway","South Korea","Spain","Sweden","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Baylor College of Medicine","Clalit Health Services","Dartmouth College","Department of Clinical Neurobiology/A230, Heidelberg University Hospital at DKFZ, Im Neuenheimer Feld 280, 69120 Heidelberg, Germany. k.khodosevich@dkfz-heidelberg.de","Fred Hutchinson Cancer Research Center","Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health","International Agency for Research on Cancer","Nanjing Medical University","National Institute of Occupational Health","National Institutes of Health","Radboud University Nijmegen","Seoul National University","Sinai Health System","Swedish Medical Center","Technical University of Munich","Umeå University","University Health Network","University of British Columbia","University of Göttingen","University of Hawai'i at Mānoa","University of Kentucky","University of Liverpool","University of Oviedo","University of Pittsburgh","University of Salzburg","University of Sheffield","University of Tampa","University of Texas MD Anderson Cancer Center","University of Toronto","Vanderbilt University","Washington State University Spokane"],"keyword":["Clonal hematopoiesis","Loss of Heterozygosity","Lung cancer risk"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"mosaic chromosomal alterations are associated with increased lung cancer risk insight from the integral ilcco cohort analysis","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/365556611/565971114.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-690226","identifier":[{"id":"37150255","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85163295925","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10435278","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-b109f2ec-ff28-4049-8675-70384d1f2cc1"],"keyword":["Clonal hematopoiesis","Loss of heterozygosity","Lung cancer risk","Mosaic chromosomal alterations"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-8DDD9C9F2F87B2E9C80599419F518F23"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/365556611/565971114.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"baylor college of medicine","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Baylor College of Medicine","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fr","key":"international agency for research on cancer","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"International Agency for Research on Cancer","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"national institutes of health","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"National Institutes of Health","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"ca","key":"university of british columbia","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of B