{"abstract":"Microbial fuel cells (MFCs) have been demonstrated as a renewable energy strategy to efficiently recover chemical energy stored in wastewater into clean electricity, yet the limited power density limits their practical application. Here, Fe-doped carbon and nitrogen (Fe@CN) nanoparticles were synthesized by a direct pyrolysis process, which was further decorated to fabricate Fe@CN carbon paper anode. The modified Fe@CN anode with a higher electrochemically active surface area was not only benefit for the adhesion of electrochemically active microorganisms (EAMs) and extracellular electron transfer (EET) between the anode and EAMs but also selectively enriched Geobacter, a typical EAMs species. Accordingly, the MFCs with Fe@CN anode successfully achieved a highest voltage output of 792.76 mV and a prolonged stable voltage output of 300 h based on the mixed culture feeding with acetate. Most importantly, the electroactive biofilms on Fe@CN anode achieved more content ratio of proteins to polysaccharides (1.40) in extracellular polymeric substances for the balance between EET and cell protection under a harsh environment. This work demonstrated the feasibility of development on anode catalysts for the elaboration of the catalytic principle about interface modification, which may contribute to the practical application of MFC in energy generation and wastewater treatment.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Cheng, Xiao-Long","Xu, Qiang","Yang, Qian-Wen","Tian, Rui-Rui","Li, Biao","Yan, Su","Zhang, Xue-Ying","Zhou, Jun","Yong, Xiao-Yu"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-316791"],"filters":{"added":"2023-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"corresponding_author":["Yong, Xiao-Yu"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Nanjing Tech University"],"keyword":["Electroactive biofilms EABs","Extracellular electron transfer EET","Microbial Fuel Cells MFCs"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"enhancing extracellular electron transfer through selective enrichment of i geobacter i with fe cn modified carbon based anode in microbial fuel cells","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/337118007/93a11e54-12a6-4135-b168-fde32ac71eba.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-316791","identifier":[{"id":"85142208891","type":"scopus"},{"id":"36396764","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-29210d48-eca8-42a8-8ada-ff8d35221991"],"keyword":["Electroactive biofilms (EABs)","Electrochemically active surface area (EASA)","Extracellular electron transfer (EET)","Fe-doped carbon and nitrogen (Fe@CN)","Microbial fuel cells (MFCs)"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-49681F31E018906B52091547F8EB9AC0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/337118007/93a11e54-12a6-4135-b168-fde32ac71eba.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"nanjing tech university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Nanjing Tech University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Environmental and Resource Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Environmental and Resource Engineering, Water Technology & Processes"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Xiao-Long","key":"chengpau","last":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Xiao-Long","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Qiang","key":"xupau","last":"Xu","name":"Xu, Qiang","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Qian-Wen","key":"yangpau","last":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qian-Wen","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rui-Rui","key":"tianpau","last":"Tian","name":"Tian, Rui-Rui","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Biao","key":"lipau","last":"Li","locid":"906c1985-9a04-443f-9b60-db5831bf520e","name":"Li, Biao","orcid":"0000-0002-7359-3690","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Su","key":"yanpau","last":"Yan","name":"Yan, Su","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Xue-Ying","key":"zhangpau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xue-Ying","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jun","key":"zhoupau","last":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jun","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Xiao-Yu","key":"yongpau","last":"Yong","name":"Yong, Xiao-Yu","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36396764"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s11356-022-24254-4","issn":["09441344","16147499"],"issn_org":["09441344"],"journal":"Environmental Science and Pollution Research","pages":"28640-28651","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Environmental Science and Pollution Research","volume":"30"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85142208891"],"sdg":["7. Affordable and Clean Energy","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-29210d48-eca8-42a8-8ada-ff8d35221991"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Enhancing extracellular electron transfer through selective enrichment of <i>Geobacter</i> with Fe@CN-modified carbon-based anode in microbial fuel cells","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-316791","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Building cluster load management to harness energy flexibility and reduce both electricity cost and carbon emissions is an important but inadequately addressed issue in the context of carbon neutrality. This study develops a multi-agent system (MAS) based coordinated optimal load scheduling strategy for building cluster load management in response to dynamic electricity price and marginal emission factor (MEF) simultaneously. The strategy effectively solves the multi-objective optimization problem of conflicts, i.e., minimizing the electricity cost, carbon emissions and peak load while maintaining a good level of users’ satisfaction with electricity use quantified by a utility function. Case study on a campus building cluster is carried out to test the strategy developed. Three demand response (DR) schemes are designed for the building cluster, i.e., price-based DR, MEF-based DR, and the price and MEF hybrid-based DR which implements the optimal scheduling strategy developed. In addition, two real scenarios with opposite correlations between dynamic electricity price and MEF, i.e., positively correlated (scenario 1) and negatively correlated (scenario 2), are extracted from German electricity market. The electricity costs, carbon emissions, peak loads, and utility of the three DR schemes in the two scenarios are critically compared. The results show that the price-based DR may result in the rise of carbon emissions, and the MEF-based DR may lead to higher electricity cost, depending on the correlation between dynamic electricity price and MEF. The optimal strategy developed can achieve a compromise between the conflicting objectives in both scenarios. Under the extremely disadvantageous situation like scenario 2, where the trends of the price and MEF are completely opposite, the price-based DR results in an increase of carbon emission of 2.78%, and the MEF-based DR leads to an increase of electricity cost of 2.63%. The hybrid-based DR can reduce the peak power by 5.54% without increasing electricity cost and carbon emissions in scenario 2. This research provides an effective optimal load scheduling strategy as well as the application guideline for building cluster DR management towards decarbonization and economic benefit.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Zhang, Hanbei","Xiao, Fu","Zhang, Chong","Li, Rongling"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-330187"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"corresponding_author":["Xiao, Fu"],"country":["Hong Kong"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Hong Kong Polytechnic University"],"keyword":["Building cluster","Demand response","Multi agent system","Multi objective optimization"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a multi agent system based coordinated multi objective optimal load scheduling strategy using marginal emission factors for building cluster demand response","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85145967427","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-330187","identifier":[{"id":"85145967427","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-5638bb39-2379-4739-a8c9-b048aa813856"],"keyword":["Building cluster","Demand response","Marginal emission factors","Multi-Agent System","multi-objective optimization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-26753F52E1508B6C2336ADF1063B86C7"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"hk","key":"hong kong polytechnic university","loc":["Hong Kong","Asia, East"],"name":"Hong Kong Polytechnic University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Civil and Mechanical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Civil and Mechanical Engineering, Energy and Services"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hanbei","key":"zhanghanbeipau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hanbei","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Fu","key":"xiaofupau","last":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Fu","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Chong","key":"zhangchongpau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chong","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rongling","key":"lironglingpau","last":"Li","locid":"3abaa1f5-4a6f-4b8c-93a4-96d52a5beef9","name":"Li, Rongling","orcid":"0000-0002-3654-8014","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.enbuild.2022.112765","issn":["03787788","18726178"],"issn_org":["03787788"],"journal":"Energy and Buildings","paperid":"112765","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Energy and Buildings","volume":"281"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85145967427"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-5638bb39-2379-4739-a8c9-b048aa813856"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"A multi-agent system based coordinated multi-objective optimal load scheduling strategy using marginal emission factors for building cluster demand response","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-330187","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Mg(BH4)2 with several polymorphs, known as a high capacity (14.9 wt.%) hydrogen storage material, has become more intriguing due to the recently found new functions of gas physisorption and ionic conductivity. Here we review the state-of-the-art on the energy related functions of Mg(BH4)2. Mg(BH4)2 tends to form the stable intermediate [B12H12]2− when the dehydrogenation temperature is above 400 °C, the strong B-B bonding of which makes the rehydrogenation condition very harsh. In contrast, lower borane intermediate [B3H8]2− facilitates the rehydrogenation even at a mild condition of 100 °C, suggesting the possibility of reversible hydrogen storage in Mg(BH4)2. The porous polymorph γ-Mg(BH4)2 shows attractive gas adsorption properties in view of its unique hydridic surface and pore shape, and potentially can be applied in hydrogen adsorption and Kr/Xe selectivity. A new diffraction-based adsorption methodology was developed to characterize adsorption thermodynamics and kinetics of γ-Mg(BH4)2, providing a novel idea for the characterization of crystalline porous materials. Moreover, the potential of Mg(BH4)2 as an electrolyte is discussed in the last part. Mg(BH4)2·THF/DME acts as a liquid electrolyte in Mg-batteries, while anion substituted or neutral molecule derivatives of Mg(BH4)2 can act as solid-state electrolyte.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Li, Xiao","Yan, Yigang","Jensen, Torben René","Filinchuk, Yaroslav","Dovgaliuk, Iurii","Chernyshov, Dmitry","He, Liqing","Li, Yongtao","Li, Hai Wen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-675971"],"filters":{"added":"2023-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Filinchuk, Yaroslav","Li, Hai Wen"],"country":["Belgium","China","France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Anhui University of Technology","European Synchrotron Radiation Facility","Sichuan University","Universite Catholique de Louvain"],"keyword":["