{"abstract":"Phototherapy has drawn increasing attention including the use of nanocarriers with high drug loading capacity and delivery efficacy for target-specific therapy. We have made use of naturally-occurring halloysite nanotubes (HNTs) to build a biomimetic nanocarrier platform for target-specific delivery of phototherapeutic agents. The HNTs were decorated with poly(sodium-p-styrenesulfonate) (PSS) to enhance the biocompatibility, and were further functionalized by lumen loading the type-II photosensitizer indocyanine green (ICG). The HNT-PSS-ICG nanocarrier, without further tethering targeting groups, was shown to associate with the membrane of giant unilamellar vesicles (GUVs) via Pickering effects. Application of HNT-PSS-ICG nanocarrier to human breast cancer cells gave rise to a cell mortality as high as 95%. The HNT-PSS-ICG nanocarrier was further coated with MDA-MB-436 cell membranes to endow it with targeting therapy performance against breast cancer, which was confirmed by in vivo experiments using breast cancer tumors in mice. The membrane-coated and biocompatible nanocarrier preferentially concentrated in the tumor tissue, and efficiently decreased the tumor volume by a combination of photodynamic and photothermal effects upon near-infrared light exposure. Our results demonstrate that the HNT-based nanocarrier by virtue of facial preparation and high loading capacity can be a promising candidate for membrane-targeting nanocarriers.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Li, Luo-Yuan","Zhou, Yi-Ming","Gao, Rong-Yao","Liu, Xiao-Chen","Du, Hui-Hui","Zhang, Jia-Ling","Ai, Xi-Cheng","Zhang, Jian-Ping","Fu, Li-Min","Skibsted, Leif Horsfelt"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-506850"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Renmin University of China"],"keyword":["Cell Membrane","Giant unilamellar vesicle","Halloysite nanotubes","Phototherapy"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"naturally occurring nanotube with surface modification as biocompatible target specific nanocarrier for cancer phototherapy","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-506850","identifier":[{"id":"85057206806","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30408640","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-be023e3b-47d3-43fa-88c6-1eb331f612c5"],"keyword":["Cell membrane","Giant unilamellar vesicle","Halloysite nanotubes","Phototherapy","Pickering effect"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-3A221408E988F86460AE8C565654D599"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"renmin university of china","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Renmin University of China","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Food Science, Design and Consumer Behavior"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Luo-Yuan","key":"liluoyuanpau","last":"Li","name":"Li, Luo-Yuan","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Yi-Ming","key":"zhouyimingpau","last":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yi-Ming","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Rong-Yao","key":"gaorongyaopau","last":"Gao","name":"Gao, Rong-Yao","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Xiao-Chen","key":"liuxiaochenpau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiao-Chen","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Hui-Hui","key":"duhuihuipau","last":"Du","name":"Du, Hui-Hui","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jia-Ling","key":"zhangjialingpau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jia-Ling","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Xi-Cheng","key":"aixichengpau","last":"Ai","name":"Ai, Xi-Cheng","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jian-Ping","key":"zhangjianpingpau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jian-Ping","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Li-Min","key":"fuliminpau","last":"Fu","name":"Fu, Li-Min","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Leif H.","key":"skibstedleifhorsfeltpau","last":"Skibsted","locid":"c8bd9975-50bb-4e25-8dd3-a3738a78ec9b","name":"Skibsted, Leif Horsfelt","name_org":"Skibsted, Leif H.","orcid":"0000-0003-1734-5016","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30408640"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.biomaterials.2018.10.046","issn":["01429612","18785905"],"issn_org":["01429612"],"journal":"Biomaterials","pages":"86-96","status":"p","title":"Biomaterials","volume":"190-191"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85057206806"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-be023e3b-47d3-43fa-88c6-1eb331f612c5"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Naturally occurring nanotube with surface modification as biocompatible, target-specific nanocarrier for cancer phototherapy","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-506850","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Hypobaric hypoxia (HH) exposure can cause serious brain injury as well as life-threatening cerebral edema in severe cases. Previous studies on the mechanisms of HH-induced brain injury have been conducted primarily using non-primate animal models that are genetically distant to humans, thus hindering the development of disease treatment. Here, we report that cynomolgus monkeys ( Macacafascicularis) exposed to acute HH developed human-like HH syndrome involving severe brain injury and abnormal behavior. Transcriptome profiling of white blood cells and brain tissue from monkeys exposed to increasing altitude revealed the central role of the HIF-1 and other novel signaling pathways, such as the vitamin D receptor (VDR) signaling pathway, in co-regulating HH-induced inflammation processes. We also observed profound transcriptomic alterations in brains after exposure to acute HH, including the activation of angiogenesis and impairment of aerobic respiration and protein folding processes, which likely underlie the pathological effects of HH-induced brain injury. Administration of progesterone (PROG) and steroid neuroprotectant 5α-androst-3β,5,6β-triol (TRIOL) significantly attenuated brain injuries and rescued the transcriptomic changes induced by acute HH. Functional investigation of the affected genes suggested that these two neuroprotectants protect the brain by targeting different pathways, with PROG enhancing erythropoiesis and TRIOL suppressing glutamate-induced excitotoxicity. Thus, this study advances our understanding of the pathology induced by acute HH and provides potential compounds for the development of neuroprotectant drugs for therapeutic treatment.","author_count":27,"contributors":["Zhang, Pei","Chen, Jie-Si","Li, Qi-Ye","Sheng, Long-Xiang","Gao, Yi-Xing","Lu, Bing-Zheng","Zhu, Wen-Bo","Zhan, Xiao-Yu","Li, Yuan","Yuan, Zhi-Bing","Xu, Gang","Qiu, Bitao","Yan, Min","Guo, Chun-Xue","Wang, You-Qiong","Huang, Yi-Jun","Zhang, Jing-Xia","Liu, Fu-Yu","Tang, Zhong-Wei","Lin, Sui-Zhen","Cooper, David N","Yang, Huan-Ming","Wang, Jian","Gao, Yu-Qi","Yin, Wei","Zhang, Guojie","Yan, Guang-Mei"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-512151"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BGI"],"keyword":["Brain injury","Cynomolgus monkeys","Gene Regulatory Networks"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"neuroprotectants attenuate hypobaric hypoxia induced brain injuries in cynomolgus monkeys","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.24272/j.issn.2095-8137.2020.012","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-512151","identifier":[{"id":"31840949","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC6956719","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85077790760","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-c6e67e69-bc97-4bf6-8c25-bac243ee2ecc"],"keyword":["Acute hypobaric hypoxia","Brain injury","Cynomolgus monkeys","Gene regulatory networks","Neuroprotectant"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-991C805D7AB535DD3E4A8B4EF483B028"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.24272/j.issn.2095-8137.2020.012","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/248820569/Neuroprotectants_attenuate_hypobaric_hypoxia_induced_brain_injuries_in_cynomolgus_monkeys_version_of_record_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-K