{"abstract":"In aquatic ecosystems, ammonium is one of the dominant substances in the effluent discharge from wastewater treatment plants and its impact has been widely explored as it is thought, in its toxic form (NH 3), to cause stress on organisms. Little is, however, known about its potential effect on the release of phosphorus (P) from the sediment. In a two-month mesocosm (150 L) experiment, we tested if high loading of ammonium promotes sediment P release and investigated the dominant underlying mechanisms. A gradient of five target ammonium loading levels was used by adding NH 4Cl fertilizer: no addition/control (N0), 3 (N1), 5 (N2), 10 (N3), and 21 (N4) mg NH 4Cl L −1 (NH 4Cl expressed as nitrogen). We found that: 1) significant sediment P release for N3 and N4 but minor release or retention for N0, N1, and N2 were detected both by the total phosphorus concentration (TP) in the overlying water and in situ measurements of diffusive gradients in thin-films (DGT) at the sediment-water interface; 2) overall, TP correlated significantly and positively with total nitrogen (TN) concentrations in the water. Further correlation and path analyses suggested that stimulated alkaline phosphatase activity (APA) was likely the dominant mechanisms behind the ammonium-induced sediment P release and decreased dissolved oxygen (DO) levels (an approximate reduction from 9.2 to 6.6 mg O2 L −1) was likely a contributing factor, particularly in the beginning of the experiment.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Ma, Shuo Nan","Wang, Hai Jun","Wang, Hong Zhu","Li, Yan","Liu, Miao","Liang, Xiao Min","Yu, Qing","Jeppesen, Erik","Søndergaard, Martin"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-246724"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"corresponding_author":["Wang, Hai Jun"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","CAS - Institute of Hydrobiology","Sino-Danish Centre for Education and Research","University of Chinese Academy of Sciences"],"keyword":["Alkaline phosphatase activity","China","Dissolved oxygen","Eutrophic lake","Global assessment","Lake sediments","Nitrogen","Phytoplankton","Sediment phosphorus release","Shallow lakes","TAIHU","WATER INTERFACE"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"high ammonium loading can increase alkaline phosphatase activity and promote sediment phosphorus release a two month mesocosm experiment","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85053049293","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-246724","identifier":[{"id":"85053049293","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000449137700037","type":"wos"},{"id":"30173099","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-cd61958b-f1d7-4d17-829f-59fff40f798a"],"keyword":["Alkaline phosphatase activity","Ammonium loading","CHINA","Dissolved oxygen","EUTROPHIC LAKE","GLOBAL ASSESSMENT","LAKE-SEDIMENTS","NITROGEN","PHYTOPLANKTON","SHALLOW LAKES","Sediment phosphorus release","TAIHU","WATER INTERFACE"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CD52B42533EE91B34E630B00E01D1A66"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"university of chinese academy of sciences","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"University of Chinese Academy of Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"cn",