{"abstract":"Background: Optic disc drusen (ODD) are seen in up to 2.4% of the general population, but the etiology and pathophysiology of the condition is still unknown. The purpose of this study was to determine the prevalence of ODD in a population-based child cohort and to determine if scleral canal diameter and fetal birth and pubertal parameters are associated with the presence of ODD. Methods: This observational, longitudinal population-based birth cohort study, with a nested case-control, included 1,406 children. Eye examinations were performed when the children were between 11 and 12 years of age. Assessment was performed of optical coherence tomography (OCT) scans from 1,304 children with gradable enhanced depth imaging scans of the optic disc. Results: ODD in one or both eyes were found in 13 (1.0 of all children. All but one of the cases were found in children with scleral canal diameter in the lowest quartile (1,182-1,399 mu m) in the nested case-control study. Children with ODD had a mean disc diameter of 1,339 mu m (interquartile range, 30 mu m), whereas it was 1,508 mu m (interquartile range, 196 mu m) in the 130 controls without ODD (P <0.001). No differences in sex, birth weight, refractive error, and Tanner stages (of puberty) were found between children with and without ODD. Conclusions: The prevalence of ODD was 1% in a large child cohort examined by OCT. ODD was found only in eyes with a narrow scleral canal, which is consistent with the hypothesis that ODD might arise as a consequence of retinal nerve fiber congestion in the scleral canal. (C) 2017 by North American Neuro-Ophthalmology Society.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Malmqvist, Lasse","Li, Xiao Qiang","Eckmann, Christina L.","Skovgaard, Anne Mette","Olsen, Else Marie","Larsen, Michael","Munch, Inger Christine","Hamann, Steffen"],"cuid":["S02-099.43-044640"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Birth Weight","Child","Cohort Studies","Cross Sectional Studies","Denmark epidemiology","Female","Follow Up Studies","Humans","Journal Article","Male","Nerve Fibers pathology","Prevalence","Retinal Ganglion Cells pathology","Tomography Optical Coherence"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"optic disc drusen in children the copenhagen child cohort 000000002000 eye study","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85068492956","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-099.43-044640","identifier":[{"id":"85068492956","type":"scopus"},{"id":"28841585","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-9902ca96-bf0c-4d56-905a-90d89f8db0d7","sdu-e817df09-0e3c-4461-b1ab-4b5f5f52e37d"],"keyword":["Birth Weight","Child","Cohort Studies","Cross-Sectional Studies","Denmark/epidemiology","Female","Follow-Up Studies","Humans","Journal Article","Male","Nerve Fibers/pathology","Optic Disk Drusen/diagnostic imaging","Optic Disk/diagnostic imaging","Prevalence","Retinal Ganglion Cells/pathology","Sclera/pathology","Tomography, Optical Coherence"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-FA5B8C7A2437D4A04806BC01E12F5488"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","