{"abstract":"Although the importance of group selection in nature is highly controversial, several researchers have argued that plant breeding for agriculture should be based on group selection, because the goal in agriculture is to optimize population production, not individual fitness. A core hypothesis behind this claim is that crop genotypes with the highest individual fitness in a mixture of genotypes will not produce the highest population yield, because fitness is often increased by \"selfish\" behaviors, which reduce population performance. We tested this hypothesis by growing 35 cultivars of spring wheat (Triticum aestivum L.) in mixtures and monocultures, and analyzing the relationship between population yield in monoculture and individual yield in mixture. The relationship was unimodal, as predicted. The highest-yielding populations were from cultivars that had intermediate fitness, and these produced, on average, 35% higher yields than cultivars with the highest fitness. It is unlikely that plant breeding or genetic engineering can improve traits that natural selection has been optimizing for millions of years, but there is unutilized potential in traits that increase crop yield by decreasing individual fitness.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Weiner, Jacob","Du, Yan-Lei","Zhang, Cong","Qin, Xiao-Liang","Li, Feng-Min"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-536883"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Journal Article"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evolutionary agroecology individual fitness and population yield in wheat i triticum aestivum i","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-536883","identifier":[{"id":"000408836600004","type":"wos"},{"id":"85028681080","type":"scopus"},{"id":"28783218","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-f063cc05-6758-4d13-b735-645f8f202401"],"keyword":["Journal Article"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5B79F9E2E90D7C88325C93826284C42D"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, Section for Organismal Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","first":"Jacob","key":"weinerpau","last":"Weiner","locid":"dd3c08ff-333b-4591-9968-3957845a6fa1","name":"Weiner, Jacob","orcid":"0000-0002-0736-7943","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Yan-Lei","key":"dupau","last":"Du","name":"Du, Yan-Lei","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Cong","key":"zhangpau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Cong","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Xiao-Liang","key":"qinpau","last":"Qin","name":"Qin, Xiao-Liang","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Feng-Min","key":"lipau","last":"Li","name":"Li, Feng-Min","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["28783218"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/ecy.1934","issn":["00129658","19399170"],"issn_org":["00129658"],"issue":"9","journal":"Ecology","pages":"2261-2266","status":"p","title":"Ecology","volume":"98"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85028681080"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-f063cc05-6758-4d13-b735-645f8f202401"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Evolutionary agroecology : individual fitness and population yield in wheat (<i>Triticum aestivum</i>)","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-536883","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"The inflammatory intestinal disorder Crohn's disease (CD) has become a health challenge worldwide. The gut microbiota closely interacts with the host immune system, but its functional impact in CD is unclear. Except for studies on a small number of CD patients, analyses of the gut microbiota in CD have used 16S rDNA amplicon sequencing. Here we employed metagenomic shotgun sequencing to provide a detailed characterization of the compositional and functional features of the CD microbiota, comprising also unannotated bacteria, and investigated its modulation by exclusive enteral nutrition (EEN). Based on signature taxa, CD microbiotas clustered into two distinct metacommunities indicating individual variability in CD microbiome structure. Metacommunity-specific functional shifts in CD showed enrichment in producers of the pro-inflammatory hexa-acylated lipopolysaccharide variant and a reduction in the potential to synthesize short chain fatty acids. Disruption of ecological networks was evident in CD, coupled with reduction in growth rates of many bacterial species. Short-term EEN elicited limited impact on the overall composition of the CD microbiota, although functional changes occurred following treatment. The microbiotas in CD patients can be stratified into two distinct metacommunities with the most severely perturbed metacommunity exhibiting functional potentials that deviate markedly from that of the healthy individuals with possible implication in relation to CD pathogenesis.","author_count":25,"contributors":["He, Qing","Gao, Yuan","Jie, Zhuye","Yu, Xinlei","Laursen, Janne Marie","Xiao, Liang","Li, Ying","Li, Lingling","Zhang, Faming","Feng, Qiang","Li, Xiaoping","Yu, Jinghong","Liu, Chuan","Lan, Ping","Yan, Ting","Liu, Xin","Xu, Xun","Yang, Huanming","Wang, Jian","Madsen, Lise","Brix, Susanne","Wang, Jianping","Kristiansen, Karsten","Jia, Huijue"],"cuid":["S02-098.83-036260"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BGI Group","The Second Affiliated Hospital of Nanjing Medical University","The Sixth Affiliated Hospital of The Sun Yat-sen University"],"keyword":["Crohn s disease","Exclusive enteral nutrition","Gut microbe","Metagenomics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"two distinct metacommunities characterize the gut microbiota in crohn s disease patients","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/gigascience/gix050","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-098.83-036260","identifier":[{"id":"000405829600001","type":"wos"},{"id":"PMC5624284","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85028373654","type":"scopus"},{"id":"28655159","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-a1ae742b-4cd0-4e4d-b862-e8fc9059b556","ku-178b607b-596e-45f8-a2c8-b61bda9af779"],"keyword":["Crohn's disease","Exclusive enteral nutrition","Gut microbe","Metagenomics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-C96B348D59D7BCE5392F06686CEFDFA3"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/gigascience/gix050","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/183802408/He_2017_Two_distinct_metacommunities.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/138627368/gix050_1_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"the sixth affiliate