{"abstract":"Cells benefit from silencing foreign genetic elements but must simultaneously avoid inactivating endogenous genes. Although chromatin modifications and RNAs contribute to maintenance of silenced states, the establishment of silenced regions will inevitably reflect underlying DNA sequence and/or structure. Here, we demonstrate that a pervasive non-coding DNA feature in Caenorhabditis elegans, characterized by 10-base pair periodic An/Tn-clusters (PATCs), can license transgenes for germline expression within repressive chromatin domains. Transgenes containing natural or synthetic PATCs are resistant to position effect variegation and stochastic silencing in the germline. Among endogenous genes, intron length and PATC-character undergo dramatic changes as orthologs move from active to repressive chromatin over evolutionary time, indicating a dynamic character to the An/Tn periodicity. We propose that PATCs form the basis of a cellular immune system, identifying certain endogenous genes in heterochromatic contexts as privileged while foreign DNA can be suppressed with no requirement for a cellular memory of prior exposure.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Frøkjær-Jensen, Christian","Jain, Nimit","Hansen, Loren","Davis, M Wayne","Li, Yongbin","Zhao, Di","Rebora, Karine","Millet, Jonathan R R M","Liu, Xiao","Kim, Stuart K","Dupuy, Denis","Jorgensen, Erik M","Fire, Andrew Z."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-432456"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["China","France","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Stanford University","Tsinghua University","University of Utah","Université de Bordeaux"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"an abundant class of non coding dna can prevent stochastic gene silencing in the c elegans germline","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-432456","identifier":[{"id":"PMC4947018","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"27374334","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84978413978","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-41468d36-e6c2-485a-97ba-672061a59bc7"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2B37B118B8BC5C5892214F3BC0BAFBD6"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Physiology of circulation, kidney and lung"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"stanford university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Stanford University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of utah","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Utah","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"cn","key":"tsinghua university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Tsinghua University","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"fr","key":"université de bordeaux","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Université de Bordeaux","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Christian","key":"frøkjærjensenpau","last":"Frøkjær-Jensen","locid":"746ca049-a3e5-458b-9bb6-2d931fa4cd98","name":"Frøkjær-Jensen, Christian","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Nimit","key":"jainpau","last":"Jain","name":"Jain, Nimit","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Loren","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","name":"Hansen, Loren","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"