ies","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-36c49d6e-6c1d-4af4-b423-3d06886e4179"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The oral and gut microbiomes are perturbed in rheumatoid arthritis and partly normalized after treatment","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-425978","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: To report atypical central serous chorioretinopathy and choroidal thickening in a patient with heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension. METHODS: A 40-year-old man with heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension presented with blurred vision in his left eye and was followed up for 1 year with clinical examination, enhanced depth optical coherence tomography, fluorescein and indocyanine green angiography, and fundus photography. RESULTS: At presentation, atypical central serous chorioretinopathy with multiple retinal pigment epithelial detachments, a thick subfoveal choroid, and dilated choroidal vessels were seen in the patient's symptomatic left eye. After treatment for pulmonary hypertension, the serous detachments disappeared and choroidal thickness gradually decreased over a period of 4 weeks and remained unchanged at 13 months of follow-up. CONCLUSION: Central serous chorioretinopathy and choroidal thickening that responded to treatment of pulmonary arterial hypertension suggest a pathophysiological link between pulmonary arterial hypertension and central serous chorioretinopathy, perhaps mediated by choroidal venous stasis.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Li, Xiao Qiang","Pryds, Anders","Carlsen, Jørn","Larsen, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-543277"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Adult","Central serous chorioretinopathy","Humans","Hypertension Pulmonary","Male","Retinal Detachment","Retinal pigment epithelium"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multifocal central serous chorioretinopathy with photoreceptor retinal pigment epithelium diastasis in heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-543277","identifier":[{"id":"25383848","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84925840865","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-fb03b046-6123-4228-b8b2-750afc5216a9"],"keyword":["Adult","Central Serous Chorioretinopathy","Choroid Diseases","Humans","Hypertension, Pulmonary","Male","Retinal Detachment","Retinal Pigment Epithelium"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-413DE5F6C18E71F7CAA2453E13E6250A"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Xiao Qiang","key":"lipau","last":"Li","name":"Li, Xiao Qiang","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anders","key":"prydspau","last":"Pryds","locid":"9ca68481-f168-4fae-a653-602836e85d90","name":"Pryds, Anders","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jørn","key":"carlsenpau","last":"Carlsen","locid":"d1394cd1-fa74-45ea-a5f4-7730f6c6de0c","name":"Carlsen, Jørn","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Michael","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"1d460214-c0db-4a15-a3a5-360c73746ed4","name":"Larsen, Michael","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["25383848"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1097/icb.0000000000000090","issn":["19351089","19371578"],"issn_org":["19351089"],"issue":"1","journal":"Retinal Cases & Brief Reports","pages":"83-7","status":"p","title":"Retinal Cases & Brief Reports","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84925840865"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-fb03b046-6123-4228-b8b2-750afc5216a9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Multifocal central serous chorioretinopathy with photoreceptor-retinal pigment epithelium diastasis in heritable pulmonary arterial hypertension","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-543277","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"Logistic regression analysis based on data from 822 Han Chinese oldest old aged 92+ demonstrated that interactions between carrying FOXO1A-266 or FOXO3-310 or FOXO3-292 and tea drinking at around age 60 or at present time were significantly associated with lower risk of cognitive disability at advanced ages. Associations between tea drinking and reduced cognitive disability were much stronger among carriers of the genotypes of FOXO1A-266 or FOXO3-310 or FOXO3-292 compared with noncarriers, and it was reconfirmed by analysis of three-way interactions across FOXO genotypes, tea drinking at around age 60, and at present time. Based on prior findings from animal and human cell models, we postulate that intake of tea compounds may activate FOXO gene expression, which in turn may positively affect cognitive function in the oldest old population. Our empirical findings imply that the health benefits of particular nutritional interventions, including tea drinking, may, in part, depend upon individual genetic profiles.","author_count":21,"contributors":["Zeng, Yi","Chen, Huashuai","Ni, Ting","Ruan, Rongping","Feng, Lei","Nie, Chao","Cheng, Lingguo","Li, Yang","Tao, Wei","Gu, Jun","Land, Kenneth C","Yashin, Anatoli","Tan, Qihua","Yang, Ze","Bolund, Lars","Yang, Huanming","Hauser, Elizabeth","Willcox, Craig D","Willcox, Bradley J","Tian, Xiao-Li","Vaupel, James W"],"cuid":["S02-091.68-030373"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Asia, South","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["China","Germany","Japan","Singapore","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BGI Group","BGI-Shenzhen","Duke University","Duke University Medical Center","Fudan University","Max Planck Institute For Demographic Research","Nanjing University","National University of Singapore","Okinawa Institute of Science and Technology Graduate University","Okinawa International University","Peking University","Renmin University of China","University of Hawaii","University of Hawaii at Manoa","Xiangtan University"],"keyword":["Aged 80 and over","Aging genetics","Alleles","Asian Continental Ancestry Group genetics","China ethnology","Cognition drug effects","Cognitive disability","Drinking Behavior","Evidence Based Medicine","FOXO genotypes","Female","Forkhead Box Protein O1","Forkhead Box Protein O3","Forkhead Transcription Factors genetics","Gene expression","Genotype","GxE interactions","Humans","Longitudinal Studies","Male","Oldest old","Phenotype","Risk Factors","Surveys and Questionnaires","Tea","Tea drinking"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"gxe interactions between foxo genotypes and tea drinking are significantly associated with cognitive disability at advanced ages in china","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/gerona/glu060","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-091.68-030373","identifier":[{"id":"24895270","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC4447795","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"84926676993","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-f580e3ba-c833-4a20-af76-01738f4c0216","sdu-5eb8f08b-4ae2-4c29-89be-49a300bca0e4"],"keyword":["Aged, 80 and over","Aging/genetics","Alleles","Asian Continental Ancestry Group/genetics","China/ethnology","Cognition Disorders/ethnology","Cognition/drug effects","Cognitive disability","Drinking Behavior","Evidence-Based Medicine","FOXO genotypes","Female","Forkhead Box Protein O1","Forkhead Box Protein O3","Forkhead Transcription Factors/genetics","Gene Expression","Genotype","GxE interactions","Humans","Longitudinal Studies","Male","Oldest old","Phenotype","Risk 