ll,"first":"Z.M.","key":"zhengzmpau","last":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Z.M.","rank":343,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"13","corresponding":null,"first":"H.L.","key":"zhuanghlpau","last":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, H.L.","rank":344,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"V.","key":"zhukovvpau","last":"Zhukov","name":"Zhukov, V.","rank":345,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"A.","key":"zichichiapau","last":"Zichichi","name":"Zichichi, A.","rank":346,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"9","corresponding":null,"first":"P.","key":"zucconppau","last":"Zuccon","name":"Zuccon, P.","rank":347,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"11","corresponding":null,"first":"C.","key":"zurbachcpau","last":"Zurbach","name":"Zurbach, C.","rank":348,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["25166975"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1103/physrevlett.110.141102","issn":["00319007","10797114"],"issn_org":["00319007"],"issue":"14","journal":"Physical Review Letters","paperid":"141102","publisher":"American Physical Society","status":"p","title":"Physical Review Letters","volume":"110"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84876003828"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-c88de339-e13f-459d-9e3c-b699219238c4"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"First result from the alpha magnetic spectrometer on the international space station : Precision measurement of the positron fraction in primary cosmic rays of 0.5-350 gev","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-243884","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Plant responses to crowding have been investigated extensively in stands of light‐demanding species, but shade‐tolerant species may react differently. In the present study, we investigated the effect of density on the mortality, size inequality, and biomass allocation of Alternanthera philoxeroides, a shade‐tolerant invasive species. Stem fragments of A. philoxeroides were grown at either low or high densities (6 vs. 24 plants per pot) under three light levels (10%, 34%, and 100% full sun). After 8 weeks, survival was 31% lower in pots with a higher initial density. Both high density and low light levels reduced plant size substantially. Mean plant biomass ranged from 0.23 g in high‐density and low‐light pots to 4.41 g in low‐density and high‐light pots. There were no strong or significant effects of density or light level on size inequality of survivors. Most of the variation in allocation and morphology in response to light level and crowding were due to plant size and allometric growth, with little evidence of true plasticity. There was a small but significant increase in shoot allocation, in the direction predicted by optimal allocation theory, at low light levels. Our results show that intense competition need not be size asymmetric, and suggest that tolerance to low light levels involves a reduction in phenotypic plasticity. Responses of the invasive A. philoxeroides to crowding may be an example of an invasive plant’s success in establishing dense stands of closely related individuals that are shade tolerant, cooperative, and follow a relatively fixed allometric trajectory with low plasticity.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Pan, Xiao-Yun","Weiner, Jacob","Li, Bo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-447233"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Ministry of Education Key Laboratory for Biodiversity Science & Ecological Engineering"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"size symmetric competition in a shade tolerant invasive plant","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/jse.12001","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-447233","identifier":[{"id":"84893333334","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-59f5a338-aed7-42a9-bdc1-9c93f939ba9a"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-6-151A4BD01ADE96DCB16F0C707D9AEA6D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/jse.12001","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"ministry of education key laboratory for biodiversity science ecological engineering","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Ministry of Education Key Laboratory for Biodiversity Science & Ecological Engineering","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, Section for Organismal Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Xiao-Yun","key":"panpau","last":"Pan","name":"Pan, Xiao-Yun","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","first":"Jacob","key":"weinerpau","last":"Weiner","locid":"dd3c08ff-333b-4591-9968-3957845a6fa1","name":"Weiner, Jacob","orcid":"0000-0002-0736-7943","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bo","key":"lipau","last":"Li","name":"Li, Bo","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/jse.12001","issn":["16744918","17596831"],"issn_org":["16744918"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journal of Systematics and Evolution","pages":"318-325","status":"p","title":"Journal of Systematics and Evolution","volume":"51"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84893333334"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-59f5a338-aed7-42a9-bdc1-9c93f939ba9a"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Size-symmetric competition in a shade-tolerant invasive plant","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-447233","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Plants produce biomass and then allocate some of this biomass to reproduction. The pattern of reproductive allocation is an important aspect of a plant’s reproductive strategy in nature and is closely linked to yield and Harvest Index in cereal crops. Recent research has concluded that reproductive allocation should be analyzed and interpreted allometrically because ratios or fractions such as Reproductive Effort or Harvest Index are size dependent. We investigated reproductive allocation of individuals in 6 varieties of Triticum (wheat) grown at a wide range of densities. We harvested leaves, stems, spikes and grains of individual plants and analyzed the relationship between grain mass and vegetative mass allometrically. The large variation in density created large variation in plant mass and reproductive output. Most of the variation in individual yield (grain mass) was due to variation in plant size. There were significant differences among the varieties in the allometric exponent (slope of log–log relationship) of grain versus vegetative mass, such that some varieties produced higher yield (and therefore had a higher Harvest Index) than others when plants were small, while others had higher yield at larger sizes. Thus, the Harvest Index and its rank among varieties changed with plant size, which puts into question the practice of selecting for Harvest Index when crop performance varies greatly among individuals, years or environments. Selection for a high Harvest Index when individuals are large may mean unintentional selection for a lower Harvest Index when individuals are smaller. We conclude that cereal breeders should focus on reproductive allometry when interpreting Harvest Index, and select for allometric patterns that are most advantageous in a given agronomic context, especially when there is large variation in productivity among individuals, locations or years.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Qin, Xiao-liang","Weiner, Jacob","Qi, Lin","Xiong, You-cai","Li, Feng-min"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-496505"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Lanzhou University"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"allometric analysis of the effects of density on reproductive allocation and harvest index in 000000000006 varieties of wheat em triticum em","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fcr.2012.12.011","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-496505","identifier":[{"id":"84874576018","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-ac8ca4ee-e936-43b8-b4cf-256935a05e65"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-00AF49271C68F9E60AEA03B5E51D598E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fcr.2012.12.011","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378429012004698","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"lanzhou university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Lanzhou University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, Section for Organismal Biology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Xiao-liang","key":"qinpau","last":"Qin","name":"Qin, Xiao-liang","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","first":"Jacob","key":"weinerpau","last":"Weiner","locid":"dd3c08ff-333b-4591-9968-3957845a6fa1","name":"Weiner, Jacob","orcid":"0000-0002-0736-7943","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lin","key":"qipau","last":"Qi","name":"Qi, Lin","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"You-cai","key":"xiongpau","last":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, You-cai","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Feng-min","key":"lipau","last":"Li","name":"Li, Feng-min","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.fcr.2012.12.011","issn":["03784290","18726852"],"issn_org":["03784290"],"journal":"Field Crops Research","pages":"162-166","status":"p","title":"Field Crops Research","volume":"144"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84874576018"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-ac8ca4ee-e936-43b8-b4cf-256935a05e65"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Allometric analysis of the effects of density on reproductive allocation and Harvest Index in 6 varieties of wheat (<em>Triticum</em>)","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-496505","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"The genome of potato, a major global food crop, was recently sequenced. The work presented here details the integration of the potato reference genome (DM) with a new STS marker based linkage map and other physical and genetic maps of potato and the closely related species tomato. Primary anchoring of the DM genome assembly was accomplished using a diploid segregating population, which was genotyped with several types of molecular genetic markers to construct a new ~936 cM linkage map comprising 2,469 marker loci. In silico anchoring approaches employed genetic and physical maps from the diploid potato genotype RH and tomato. This combined approach has allowed 951 superscaffolds to be ordered into pseudomolecules corresponding to the 12 potato chromosomes. These pseudomolecules represent 674 Mb (~93%) of the 723 Mb genome assembly and 37,482 (~96%) of the 39,031 predicted genes. The superscaffold order and orientation within the pseudomolecules is closely collinear with independently constructed high density linkage maps. Comparisons between marker distribution and physical location reveal regions of greater and lesser recombination, as well as regions exhibiting significant segregation distortion. The work presented here has led to a greatly improved ordering of the potato reference genome superscaffolds into chromosomal 'pseudomolecules'.","author_count":45,"contributors":["Sharma, Sanjeev Kumar","Bolser, Daniel","de Boer, Jan","Sønderkær, Mads","Amoros, Walter","Carboni, Martin Federico","D'Ambrosio, Juan Martín","de la Cruz, German","Di Genova, Alex","Douches, David S","Eguiluz, Maria","Guo, Xiao","Guzman, Frank","Hackett, Christine A","Hamilton, John P","Li, Guangcun","Li, Ying","Lozano, Roberto","Maass, Alejandro","Marshall, David","Martinez, Diana Sainz","McLean, Karen","Mejía, Nilo","Milne, Linda","Munive, Susan","Nagy, Istvan","Ponce, Olga","Ramirez, Manuel","Simon, Reinhard","Thomson, Susan J","Torres, Yerisf","Waugh, Robbie","Zhang, Zhonghua","Huang, Sanwen","Visser, Richard G F","Bachem, Christian W B","Sagredo, Boris","Feingold, Sergio E","Orjeda, Gisella","Veilleux, Richard E","Bonierbale, Merideth","Jacobs, Jeanne M E","Milbourne, Dan","Martin, David Michael Alan","Bryan, Glenn J"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-087830"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"construction of reference chromosome scale pseudomolecules for potato integrating the potato genome with genetic and physical maps","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1534/g3.113.007153","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-087830","identifier":[{"id":"000327241900011","type":"wos"},{"id":"24062527","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84889019837","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-81f090e0-8e52-4866-b964-5e2f9739960b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DCD5AADDE4F729CE528D04F34912C3CC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1534/g3.113.007153","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science (ENG)","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Chemistry and Bioscience","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Chemistry and Bioscience, Section of Biotechnology","Aa